 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Gradient of RBF Field](14702-gradient-of-rbf-field.md)
-A fragment program that reconstructs scalar field values from procedurally encoded Radial Basis Functions (RBFs) in a single pass, removing multipass-related parts for readability.
+Computes the scalar field value and its spatial gradient analytically in a single fragment shader pass by evaluating a sum of Gaussian Radial Basis Functions (RBFs). It differs from its parent by operating in 3D model space using standard Gaussian RBFs rather than 2D conic projections, and by explicitly computing the gradient $\nabla f(x)$ in parallel with the field value $f(x)$. Choose it when reconstructing procedurally encoded scalar fields for interactive visualization and isosurface rendering via accurate normal estimation.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per Gaussian per pixel. The operations involve fixed-size matrix multiplications (2x2) and exponentials.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory.
-- **Performance:** The conic variant is generally faster as it avoids computing the determinant and inverse of the covariance matrix at runtime, using pre-computed conic values. [S1, unofficial]
-- **Strengths:** Provides exact gradients for backpropagation in Gaussian Splatting. The conic variant is efficient for rasterization.
-- **Limitations:** Does not compute gradient w.r.t. rescale factor. Requires careful handling of degenerate covariances.
-- **When to use:** During the training phase of Gaussian Splatting when gradients are needed for optimization of mean and covariance parameters.
-- **When not to use:** During inference/rendering if only the density value is needed (use [2D Gaussian Probability Density (Standard)](3466-2d-gaussian-probability-density-standard.md) or [2D Gaussian Probability Density (Conic)](3475-2d-gaussian-probability-density-conic.md)).
+Not given in the sources.
 
 ## Background
-This method is part of the gradient computation pipeline for 3D Gaussian Splatting, specifically for the 2D projection step. It is implemented in the unofficial Taichi-based implementation of 3D Gaussian Splatting. [S1, unofficial]
